window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][38] = "<p><em>Blast radius and independence (C17.2).</em> The change was global in effect. The recovery path shared fate with the failure: Meta says the loss of DNS broke many internal tools and that both its primary and its out-of-band network access were down (some press reports add that physical access systems were affected, which Meta's post does not say). A principle: recovery tools should not depend on the thing they recover, and even an out-of-band path needs its own independence.</p>\n<p><em>Rollback and drills (C17.5).</em> Meta says it had never run a storm drill that simulated losing its global backbone, although its other storm drills prepared it to bring load back carefully, and recovery could not be undone with a remote command. Staged rollout of changes to critical systems, an audit step that can prevent a command from taking down everything, and regular drills for the worst case are the practices that this case argues for. The postmortem culture (C17.5) is visible in Meta's published account, which explained the cause in plain terms.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> The internal reasoning behind the design, the exact tool that failed to prevent the command, and details of the physical recovery. The case supports a structural analysis only, and no judgment of any individual.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could have faced; not a record of Meta's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep the DNS withdrawal rule as designed</td>\n<td>Prevents stale or wrong answers in a local failure</td>\n<td>Amplifies a global failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Keep the rule but add a global guard: if all locations would withdraw at once, keep some answering</td>\n<td>Protects against both</td>\n<td>Complexity; a harder rule to reason about</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Separate recovery tools and access onto an independent network</td>\n<td>A way back when the main network fails</td>\n<td>Cost of a second network and its upkeep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Gate backbone commands with a simulation or a staged apply, and drill backbone loss</td>\n<td>Prevents the trigger</td>\n<td>Slower maintenance; engineering effort</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Each option adds cost or complexity to guard against a rare event. The cost of the event (billions of users, six hours) is the comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Meta restored the backbone and brought services back gradually, and its account says it will review the incident and strengthen testing, drills and resilience, and look for ways to simulate events like this <code>[V: Meta's post]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: services restored on the evening of 4 October (Cloudflare saw routes return from about 21:20 UTC; press reports say about six hours), with Meta's detailed explanation published on 5 October <code>[V: Meta's post; Cloudflare]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> With an independent recovery path the outage might have been shorter; with a global guard on the DNS rule the services might have stayed partly reachable. A reader should avoid hindsight: the individual pieces were reasonable, and the failure came from how they interacted. The method to carry away: draw the request path and the recovery path, ask what each protective rule does if it triggers everywhere, keep the way back independent of the thing that can fail, and rehearse the worst case.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c21-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C21-01 · Beginner · technical · TE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What happens when you open an app and your feed loads?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The app asks the server for the data and shows it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Walks the path: device, name lookup, connection, server, database, response, rendering.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Says where time and failure concentrate, mentions caching and percentiles, and ties the path to what the user sees when a hop fails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll walk the path in the order it happens, and say where it can go wrong.</p>\n<p>First, the app on my phone decides what to ask for: the latest items for my account. It needs the address of the company's server, so it asks the Domain Name System, which turns a name into a numeric address. A copy of recent answers is kept on the phone or the network, so this is usually fast. Then the phone opens a secure connection to the server's front door, which is usually a load balancer that spreads requests among many servers, often behind a content delivery network that already holds common files near me.</p>\n<p>The request reaches an application server, which checks who I am, then asks other services and a database for what goes into my feed: my follows, recent posts, ranking, maybe ads. It may use a cache for popular items. It assembles a response and sends it back, and my phone draws it, loading images as I scroll.</p>\n<p><em>Where time goes.</em> On a good network, the server and database work dominates; on a poor network, connection setup and transfer dominate; on an old phone, drawing can. So when someone says it is slow I ask which hop and at which percentile, because the median can look fine while one user in twenty waits several seconds.</p>\n<p><em>Where it fails, and what the user sees.</em> If the name lookup fails, nothing loads. If the database is slow, the feed is slow. If one service (say ads) is down, a well-designed app shows the feed without ads and not a blank screen: graceful degradation. The thing I would check is what each hop shows the user when it fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Where would you look first if the feed were slow, and why not the average? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would look at the 95th-percentile load time, split by hop: name lookup and connection, server compute, database, transfer and rendering. The average hides the slow minority, who are often the ones complaining, on weak networks and older phones. If the median is 1.2 seconds and the 95th percentile is 6 seconds, the typical user is fine and one in twenty waits five times as long. I would find which hop contributes most to the slow group and ask engineering what is feasible there before proposing any change.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What does the user see when a hop fails, and what should they see? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Often a blank screen or a spinner that never ends, which feels like the app is broken. A better design shows the last cached feed with a small notice that it could not refresh, keeps a retry in one tap and loads parts independently so that a failed image does not block text. I would measure the error rate per hop and the share of sessions that show a degraded state, and I would set a rule that a failure in a non-essential service must not block the core feed.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c21-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C21-02 · Beginner · technical · TE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Explain to a non-technical stakeholder what an API is.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's how programs talk to each other.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives a plain definition, an analogy and a product example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds the contract idea, versioning, and why it matters to the business (partners, speed, risk), and says one tradeoff.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> An API is a menu of requests that one piece of software offers to another. Like a restaurant menu, it says what you can ask for, in what form, and what you will get back, without you needing to know how the kitchen works.</p>\n<p>An example from a product: when a travel app shows the weather, it does not measure the weather. It sends a request to a weather company's API, &quot;forecast for this city tomorrow,&quot; and gets back a small, structured answer that the app displays. When you pay in an online shop, the shop does not handle your card; it calls a payment company's API, &quot;charge this amount,&quot; and gets back &quot;approved&quot; or &quot;declined.&quot;</p>\n<p>Why this matters for the business. First, it lets us build on others' work and lets others build on ours, which is how platforms grow. Second, an API is a <em>contract</em>: once partners build on it, we cannot change it casually, so changing it is a planned, announced process with versions. Third, it is a place where reliability and security are decided: every request is a door, and each needs a lock, a limit on how often it can be used, and monitoring.</p>\n<p>One tradeoff to know: making an API simple for partners means committing to keep it working, which limits how freely our own engineers can reshape the system behind it. So I ask what the contract is, who depends on it, and what it costs to change.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is versioning, and why does it matter to the business? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A version is a labeled form of the contract, so that changes can be introduced without breaking people who depend on the old one. Adding a field is usually safe; removing or renaming one breaks callers. Partners build products on an API, so a breaking change costs them engineering time and may make them leave. The business reason is trust and speed: a stable, versioned API lets partners integrate quickly and invest, and a careless change can cost more than the feature was worth. I would ask how long old versions are supported and how partners are told.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is one tradeoff of offering an API? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It speeds growth through partners and creates obligations. Once others rely on the API, the company must keep it stable, secure and documented, which limits how freely it can change the internals, and it exposes a surface that can be abused, so limits on request rates and authentication are needed. A private interface can change tomorrow; a public one must be supported for years. The decision to open an API should weigh partner value against the maintenance and security cost.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c21-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C21-03 · Intermediate · technical · TE · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Explain on-device versus cloud processing for a voice assistant, including privacy and latency.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;On-device is more private and cloud is more powerful.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares latency, privacy, capability, cost and offline use.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies latency on good and poor networks, proposes a hybrid with routing and disclosure, and treats sensitive requests specially.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real system. <em>On-device processing</em> means the phone does the work itself; <em>cloud processing</em> means it sends the audio or its transcript to a server and gets the answer back.</p>\n<p><em>Privacy.</em> On the device, the data never leaves, which is the strongest guarantee and a trust signal. In the cloud, the data is sent and handled by a server, so the company must justify what it collects, how long it keeps it, and who can see it. Users should be told which happens and given control.</p>\n<p><em>Latency.</em> The device has no network wait, but its processor is slower than a server's. Suppose (invented) on-device compute takes 150 ms; the cloud computes in 40 ms but needs a round trip of 60 ms and 20 ms to upload on a good connection, 120 ms in all; on a poor connection with a 400 ms round trip it is 460 ms, and offline it fails. So the cloud is slightly faster when the network is good and much slower when it is not; on-device is predictable.</p>\n<p><em>Capability.</em> The cloud can run larger models and use more data, so it handles hard requests better; the device is limited by memory and battery. Suppose easy requests (80% of traffic) are handled at about 95% accuracy on the device and hard ones at 70%, against 90% for hard requests in the cloud.</p>\n<p><em>Cost.</em> The cloud costs the company per request (say $0.002), which at a billion requests a day is $2 million a day; the device shifts cost to the user's battery and the model's size on the phone.</p>\n<p><em>My recommendation.</em> A hybrid: do the easy 80% on the device and send only the hard 20% to the cloud, with a short routing step. In my numbers that gives an average latency near 146 ms, accuracy near 94% (against 90% for device only and about 95% for cloud only), and a fifth of the cloud cost. I would keep sensitive categories on the device whatever their difficulty, tell users plainly what is sent, and measure the share routed to the cloud. What would change my view: on-device quality closing the gap, which would move more work to the device.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that technical-fluency prompts at Apple concern on-device processing and privacy <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product's design, numbers or data handling.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you quantify the latency difference on good and poor networks? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With round, invented figures. On-device recognition might respond in 150 to 300 milliseconds regardless of the network. A cloud round trip might take 400 to 800 milliseconds on a good connection and two to four seconds on a poor one, or fail. So for short commands such as set a timer, on-device is both faster and more reliable, and for complex requests that need a large model or fresh data, the cloud may be necessary. A hybrid routes simple intents on-device and complex ones to the cloud, and tells the user when something is sent.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you treat sensitive requests? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With the strongest protection by default. Requests about health, finance, messages or location would be handled on the device where possible; where the cloud is needed, the audio or text would be encrypted in transit, kept only as long as needed to answer, not used for training without consent, and clearly indicated to the user. I would give a setting to keep everything on-device with a clear statement of what stops working, and have privacy review the routing rules. The measure is the share of sensitive requests handled locally and complaints about privacy.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c21-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C21-04 · Intermediate · technical · TE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Customers say a page is slow. How do you work with engineers to find out why and what to do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask engineering to make it faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for measurements by hop and percentile, finds the biggest contributor, and decides on a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses a latency budget, checks the slow devices and networks, considers side effects and measures the effect on users.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd turn &quot;slow&quot; into numbers before asking for any fix. I'd ask engineering for the load time at the median and the 95th percentile, split by hop: name lookup and connection, server compute, database, transfer, and rendering. I'd also ask who is slow: which devices, networks and countries, because averages hide the slow minority, and the customers who complain are often in it.</p>\n<p>Then I'd find the largest contributor at the percentile that matters. Suppose the tail is 5.2 seconds and 2.0 of it is one database query. By Amdahl's law, speeding up a hop that is 5% of the time can save at most 5%, so I would not start with the front-end polish. I'd ask the engineer what is slow about that query; a common answer is a missing index, a cheap fix. I'd also ask about image sizes (transfer) and whether any result could be cached and how stale it may be.</p>\n<p>I'd put a target in writing: a 95th-percentile load time under 3 seconds on a mid-range phone on a typical mobile connection, not &quot;faster.&quot; I'd ask what the fixes cost, what they risk (a cache can show stale data, an index slows writes slightly), and how we will roll them out with a flag and a holdback so that we can measure the effect on bounce and conversion, not just on load time.</p>\n<p>Finally I'd set a guardrail so that speed does not come at the cost of correctness, and I'd re-measure after each change. What I would not do: assign a rewrite without knowing where the time goes.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What is a latency budget and how would you use it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A target for the total time split across the steps that make it up. For a page with a 2.5-second target at the 95th percentile, I might budget 400 milliseconds for network and connection, 300 for the server, 200 for the database, 800 for rendering and 800 as margin. Each team then owns a slice, and when the page exceeds the target, the budget shows which slice overran. It also gives the PM a way to talk about tradeoffs: adding a feature that costs 150 milliseconds of rendering uses a fifth of that slice and must be weighed against its value.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you measure the effect of the fix and its side effects? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Release the fix behind a flag to a random group and compare the 95th-percentile load time by device and network, plus conversion and bounce rate. Check side effects: a cache that speeds the page may show stale content, a lighter page may drop features, and a compressed image may look worse. The guardrails are error rates and cost per request. If the median improves and the 95th percentile does not, the slowest users have not been helped, so I would find what is different about them.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c21-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C21-05 · Hard · technical · TE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Users say that after saving a change to their profile, they sometimes see the old version. What could cause this, and what would you decide?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's a bug; ask engineering to fix it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names caching and copies of data as causes, and explains staleness.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes causes (browser or app cache, server cache, read replica lag), sizes the problem, proposes read-your-own-writes and design options with tradeoffs, and sets a measure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> This is a classic symptom of data that exists in more than one place, so one copy is updated before the others. I'd list where copies live, because the cause depends on which one is stale.</p>\n<p><em>Possible causes.</em> (1) <em>The client's cache:</em> the app or browser keeps the old profile and shows it until it refreshes. (2) <em>A server-side cache:</em> the application stores a copy of the profile for speed, and the write did not clear it, or the cache's lifetime has not expired. (3) <em>Read replicas:</em> to handle load, the database keeps copies that serve reads, and a copy can lag behind the main one by a short time (eventual consistency); if the page after saving reads from a lagging copy, the user sees the old data. (4) <em>A queue:</em> if the write is processed asynchronously, the change may not have been applied yet.</p>\n<p><em>Size it.</em> Ask for the rate and timing. Suppose (invented) 200,000 profile saves a day, and 5% of users view their profile within two seconds of saving, which is about 10,000 users a day who could see stale data if replica lag is about two seconds. That is a visible, trust-damaging number: people think their change was lost, and some will save again or contact support.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em> (A) <em>Read-your-own-writes:</em> for a few seconds after a user writes, route their reads to the main database or the fresh cache. Cost: a small extra load on the main database (here 10,000 reads a day, negligible) and a little logic. (B) <em>Show the new data immediately on the client,</em> then reconcile (optimistic update). Cost: a brief inconsistency if the save fails, which needs a clear error. (C) <em>Shorten cache lifetimes or clear the cache on write.</em> Cost: less speed benefit. (D) <em>Accept it:</em> acceptable for data like a follower count, not for something the user just changed.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> A and B together for user-initiated changes (the user must see their own change at once), and keep the long cache lifetimes for other users' views of the profile, where a delay of seconds is harmless. Measure: the rate of &quot;I saved but see the old value&quot; support contacts and a client check of whether the displayed value matches the saved one. The lesson to say aloud: speed comes from copies, and copies can disagree, so the product decides where staleness is acceptable.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What design options deliver read-your-own-writes, and what do they cost? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Three common ones. After a write, read that user's data from the primary copy for a few seconds, which costs extra load on the primary. Return the updated record in the response to the write and update the client's cache directly, which needs client changes. Attach a version number to the write and refuse to show data older than that version, which adds complexity. Each trades consistency for load or engineering effort. For a profile that people check right after editing, the second is often the cheapest and most visible fix.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you size the problem and decide how much to invest? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By measuring it. Instrument the share of reads within five seconds of a write that return the old version, and the number of support contacts about changes not saving. If 2% of edits show a stale profile at first view, that is thousands of confusing moments and worth fixing; if it is 0.01%, a copy change such as Your changes are saved and may take a moment to appear may be enough. I would set a target, for example under 0.1% of post-write reads stale, and review it after the fix.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c21-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C21-06 · Hard · technical · TE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A messaging app wants to set reliability targets. How would you approach it, and what would you commit to for sending messages versus loading media?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;As reliable as possible, five nines.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets different targets for different functions, using the cost of failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes downtime for each target, compounds dependencies, defines an SLO and error budget with a policy, and designs graceful degradation and retries with idempotency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd begin with what a failure costs the user, because a single target for everything wastes effort. Sending a message is the core promise; if it fails, people lose trust fast. Loading a photo that arrives a moment late is an annoyance.</p>\n<p><em>Targets and what they mean.</em> A month has about 43,834 minutes. A target of 99.95% for sending allows 0.0005 × 43,834 = about 22 minutes of failure a month. A target of 99.5% for loading media allows 219 minutes, about 3.6 hours. I would not say &quot;five nines&quot; without a price: each extra nine costs more redundancy and complexity, so I'd ask engineering what 99.95% costs against 99.9% (44 minutes) and compare it with what an extra 22 minutes of failures cost in trust.</p>\n<p><em>Dependencies.</em> Sending depends on several services (authentication, the message store, the delivery service); in a chain, availability multiplies. Three services at 99.95%, 99.95% and 99.9% give about 99.8%, which is 88 minutes of failure, worse than the target. So either the chain must be shortened (can delivery be asynchronous?) or each part must be better.</p>\n<p><em>Design for the failure.</em> Make sending resilient: save the message on the device, show it as &quot;sending,&quot; and retry in the background until the server confirms, with an idempotency key so that a retry never produces a duplicate message. For media, load a low-resolution preview first and fetch the full file when possible, so a slow media service degrades the experience without breaking the conversation.</p>\n<p><em>SLO and error budget.</em> Define the objective in terms users feel: 99.95% of send attempts are accepted within two seconds, measured monthly. The budget is about 22 minutes. A policy decided in advance: if less than a quarter of the budget remains, pause risky launches and focus on reliability; if the budget is spent, only fixes ship until it recovers.</p>\n<p><em>Measures.</em> Successful sends per attempt, the 95th and 99th percentile send time, duplicate message rate (should be zero), and how long messages sit in &quot;sending.&quot; What would change my approach: evidence that outages in one region hurt a particular market much more, which would justify regional redundancy there.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do 99.95% and 99.5% mean, and why would the targets differ? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;In a month of about 43,834 minutes, 99.95% allows about 22 minutes of failure and 99.5% allows about 219 minutes. Sending a message is the core promise, and a failure means a lost conversation, so it justifies the tighter target and the engineering cost of reaching it. Loading media that arrives a moment late is a smaller harm, so a looser target saves effort for work that matters more. Each target also has a price: going from 99.5% to 99.95% typically needs redundancy and more careful releases, and I would ask engineering what it costs.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What error-budget policy and retry design would you propose? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The budget is the allowed failure, here 22 minutes a month for sending. A policy: if half the budget is used in the first week, freeze risky releases and focus on reliability until it recovers. For retries, every send carries an idempotency key, so that a retry after a timeout does not create a duplicate message. If sending fails, the app queues the message on the device, shows a clear sending state and retries with increasing delays. This is graceful degradation: the user sees that the message is pending, not a silent loss.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c21-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C21-07 · Hard · technical · TE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Engineering says a change requires a database migration with two hours of downtime. How do you evaluate it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do it at night and tell customers,&quot; or &quot;downtime is unacceptable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the cost of downtime against the cost of avoiding it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Asks the ten questions, computes cost and the effect on the reliability budget and contracts, knows the expand-and-contract alternative, and plans rollback and communication.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat it as a cost and risk decision and start with questions to engineering (FW-C21-02): why is downtime needed, what changes in the data, what is the riskiest step, and what is the rollback if it fails halfway? Is there a way to migrate without downtime?</p>\n<p><em>Cost of the downtime.</em> Suppose (invented) the product earns $20,000 an hour at peak. A two-hour window at 03:00, when traffic is about 15% of peak, costs about 2 × $20,000 × 0.15 = $6,000 in lost sales, plus support contacts and some trust. That is small.</p>\n<p><em>Cost of avoiding it.</em> The alternative is a staged &quot;expand and contract&quot; migration: add the new structure beside the old, write to both, copy the old data in the background, switch reads to the new, then remove the old. It avoids downtime and adds a few weeks; suppose three person-weeks at $15,000 each, $45,000, plus more moving parts and more risk of a subtle bug during the dual-write period.</p>\n<p><em>Reliability budget and contracts.</em> At a 99.9% monthly objective the budget is 43.8 minutes; two hours is 120 minutes, about 2.7 times the budget. If customers have service agreements, check whether planned maintenance is excluded and how much notice is required; some enterprise contracts forbid planned downtime in business hours or require notice. So the cash comparison ($6,000 against $45,000) is not the whole story: the budget and contracts may force the staged approach.</p>\n<p><em>Plan either way.</em> Rehearse the migration on a copy of production data and time it (two hours is an estimate, not a measurement). Set a go or no-go point and a rollback that is tested. Take a verified backup. Choose the quietest window and tell customers in advance with a status page. Have support ready. Define a trigger for abort (for example, if the copy is not 60% complete by the midpoint).</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> If the contracts allow notice and the window is quiet, accept the planned two hours, with the rehearsal and rollback plan. If contracts or the budget forbid it, or if the data is critical and rollback is hard, fund the staged migration. What would change my mind: a rehearsal showing the migration takes six hours, which would make the staged route clearly better.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What questions would you ask engineering, and what is the expand-and-contract alternative? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Why downtime is needed, what changes in the data, how much data and how long it takes (a range), the riskiest step, how it was tested on production-sized data, the rollback and the point of no return, dependencies on other systems, and whether there is a way to avoid downtime. The alternative, expand and contract, adds the new structure alongside the old one, writes to both, backfills existing data, switches reads to the new structure, and only then removes the old one. It takes more steps and time and often avoids downtime entirely, so I would ask whether the extra weeks cost less than the two hours.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you plan the rollback and the communication? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With checkpoints and a verified backup. Before starting, confirm that a restore from backup works and set go or no-go checks at each stage, with the point of no return named. Announce a week before through the status page and direct messages, schedule it in the lowest-traffic window, and prepare a message for each possible outcome. Cost: at $20,000 an hour at peak and 15% of peak at 3 a.m., two hours costs about $6,000 in lost revenue, but if customers have a 99.9% availability commitment, which allows about 44 minutes a month, two hours breaches it, and service credits or contract terms may cost more. I would check contracts before choosing the window.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c21-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-01 · Draw the request path</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A user taps &quot;Send $20&quot; in a payments app. Draw the request path in hops, say what can fail at each, what the user should see, and add up a latency budget given: lookup 50 ms, connection 150 ms, server and fraud check 250 ms, database write 120 ms, response and render 180 ms.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A payments feature review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C21-01.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Hops: device, name lookup, secure connection, load balancer, application server, fraud-check service, database, payment partner, response. Failures: lookup or connection (nothing happens; show &quot;check your connection&quot; and keep the amount); server or fraud check slow or down (show &quot;processing,&quot; retry with the same idempotency key); database write fails (do not show success; the payment must not be marked sent); partner declines (show the reason); response lost after success (the user may tap again, so an idempotency key must make the repeat safe and show the original result). Budget: 50 + 150 + 250 + 120 + 180 = 750 ms, under a one-second target; the server and fraud step is the largest at 250 ms.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run the fraud check asynchronously for low-risk amounts to bring the total under 500 ms.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Asynchronous checks lower latency and raise the risk of completing a payment that is later reversed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-02 · Explain it in 90 seconds</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a 90-second plain-language explanation (definition, analogy, product example, one tradeoff) for each of: (a) a cache; (b) a database index; (c) an API.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A stakeholder meeting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Section 4, explain-a-concept type.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) A cache keeps a copy of data close to where it is needed, like keeping popular books on a front shelf; a shopping page loads faster because product details are served from the copy; the tradeoff is that the copy can be out of date. (b) An index lets the database find rows without reading every one, like the index of a book; a search on category and city goes from reading two million rows to a direct lookup; the tradeoff is extra storage and slightly slower writes. (c) An API is a menu of requests one program offers another; a weather app asks a weather service for tomorrow's forecast; the tradeoff is that once others build on it, it cannot be changed casually.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use an analogy from your own product's domain.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> An analogy simplifies and can mislead; say where it stops fitting.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-03 · Percentiles</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Twenty response times in milliseconds: 120, 130, 140, 150, 150, 160, 170, 180, 190, 200, 210, 220, 240, 260, 300, 350, 420, 800, 1,500, 3,200. Compute the mean, the median, the 95th percentile (nearest-rank method) and the maximum, and say which you would report.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A latency review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C21.2 and C12.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The mean is 9,090 ÷ 20 = 454.5 ms. The median is the average of the 10th and 11th values: (200 + 210) ÷ 2 = 205 ms. The 95th percentile by nearest rank is the 19th value: 1,500 ms. The maximum is 3,200 ms. The mean (454.5) is more than double the median (205) because a few slow responses pull it up; it describes nobody's experience. Report the median and the 95th percentile, and set the target on the 95th (and the 99th for critical paths).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Report the share of responses over a threshold (for example, 3 of 20 take over 400 ms, or 15%).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Small samples make percentiles noisy; use many requests in practice.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-04 · Cache arithmetic</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A cache answers in 5 ms and the backend in 150 ms. Compute the average latency at hit rates of 80% and 95%. At a 96% hit rate, what are the 95th and 99th percentile latencies?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Sizing a cache.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> At 80%: 0.8 × 5 + 0.2 × 150 = 34 ms. At 95%: 0.95 × 5 + 0.05 × 150 = 12.25 ms. At 96%, 4% of requests miss: the 95th percentile falls within the hits (96% are fast), so it is about 5 ms; the 99th percentile falls within the misses, so it is 150 ms. The average improves steadily with hit rate, but the 99th percentile stays at the backend's speed until the hit rate exceeds 99%. If the target is on the tail, the cache must be very effective or the backend must be faster.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Improve the backend's own latency for misses (an index, a faster query) in addition to raising the hit rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A larger cache costs memory; compare with the value of the faster tail.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-05 · A LEFT JOIN that behaves like an inner join</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Tables: users u1, u2, u3. Orders: o1 (user u1, paid), o2 (u1, refunded), o3 (u3, paid). Query: <code>SELECT u.id, COUNT(o.id) FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE o.status = 'paid' GROUP BY u.id</code>. What does it return? What did the analyst intend (paid orders per user, including zero)? How do you fix it?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Reading a query.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C21-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps all users, with missing order columns filled with nulls for users with no orders. But the <code>WHERE o.status = 'paid'</code> then removes every row where the order status is null or not paid, which removes u2 (no orders) entirely and removes u1's refunded row. The result: u1 with 1 and u3 with 1; u2 is missing. The intended result: u1: 1, u2: 0, u3: 1. Fix: move the filter into the join condition (<code>ON o.user_id = u.id AND o.status = 'paid'</code>) so that users without paid orders are kept with a count of 0. The lesson: a filter on the right-hand table in <code>WHERE</code> turns a left join into an inner join.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Count users from a separate list and subtract those with paid orders.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Dropping zero-order users can overstate an average (averages over only those with orders).</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-06 · Chain availability</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A feature depends on three services in series with availabilities 99.95%, 99.9% and 99.99%. Compute the chain's availability and the monthly downtime (43,834 minutes in a month). Does it meet a 99.9% objective, and what would you change?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Setting a target.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C21-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The chain: 0.9995 × 0.999 × 0.9999 = 0.9984, so about 99.84%, which allows (1 − 0.9984) × 43,834 = about 70 minutes of downtime a month. A 99.9% objective allows 43.8 minutes, so the chain misses it by about 26 minutes. The weakest link (99.9%) contributes most of the downtime. Options: improve that service's reliability, add a second provider or a fallback for it, remove it from the critical path (make it asynchronous or optional), or cache its results so brief outages are masked.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Lower the promise to 99.8% if the cost of reaching 99.9% exceeds the cost of the extra downtime.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Redundancy adds cost and complexity; do the arithmetic of what an outage costs.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-07 · On-device, cloud or hybrid for photo search</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A photo-search feature: on-device takes 300 ms; the cloud takes 80 ms compute plus a 100 ms network round trip (180 ms in all) and costs $0.003 per request. Easy queries are 70% of traffic: accuracy 92% on-device and 93% in the cloud. Hard queries are 30%: 60% on-device and 88% in the cloud. Compute the accuracy of each design, the average latency of a hybrid (easy on the device, hard in the cloud with a 20 ms routing step), and the cloud cost at 500 million requests a day for all-cloud and for hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A feature design.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C21-07 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Accuracy: on-device only 0.7 × 92% + 0.3 × 60% = 82.4%; cloud only 0.7 × 93% + 0.3 × 88% = 91.5%; hybrid 0.7 × 92% + 0.3 × 88% = 90.8%. Hybrid latency: easy 300 ms; hard 20 + 180 = 200 ms; average 0.7 × 300 + 0.3 × 200 = 270 ms (cloud only is 180 ms; on-device only 300 ms). Cost: all-cloud 500,000,000 × $0.003 = $1.5 million a day; hybrid sends 30%: $0.45 million. The hybrid keeps 90.8% accuracy (0.7 points below cloud only) at 30% of the cloud cost, but is slower than cloud only on average (270 against 180 ms). If latency is the priority, the cloud wins; if cost and privacy matter, the hybrid does. Say which goal you choose.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run easy queries on-device and only send a compact summary of hard ones to reduce the privacy exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Hybrid adds routing logic and two code paths to maintain.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-08 · Read the design document</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A design document proposes to &quot;move user preferences to a new service.&quot; List the effort drivers you would look for, ten questions you would ask (use FW-C21-02), and the two you would ask first.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A design review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C21-08.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Effort drivers: a new data model and a migration of existing preferences; the other teams and clients that read preferences (dependencies and old app versions); security and privacy review (preferences may be personal data); scale (reads on every page load); rollout and rollback; ongoing ownership. The two questions to ask first: &quot;What happens to a user's experience while the migration is under way, and how do we roll back if it fails?&quot; and &quot;Who reads these preferences today, including old app versions, and how do they switch?&quot; The rest from the list: riskiest part, request sent twice, freshness, cost at volume, load at ten times, what we are not building.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Ask for a one-page plan for the migration with a rehearsal date.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Too many questions slow the review; pick the few that expose the most risk.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-09 · Idempotency arithmetic</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A payments app processes 1,000,000 payments a day of $50 each. 0.5% of requests are retried because of network failures, and without safeguards 30% of retried requests are processed twice. Compute the duplicate charges per day and their value. How does an idempotency key change this? What does the user see on a retry?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A payments design.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C21.3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Retries: 1,000,000 × 0.5% = 5,000 a day. Duplicates: 5,000 × 30% = 1,500 a day, worth 1,500 × $50 = $75,000 a day of erroneous charges, plus refunds, support and trust cost. With an idempotency key, the server recognizes a retry of the same request and returns the original result without charging again, so duplicates fall to about zero. On a retry the user sees the original confirmation (not an error or a second charge). Also decide how long keys are remembered (for example, 24 hours).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Disable the button after the first tap, which reduces user-caused duplicates but does not help network retries.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Remembering keys costs storage and needs care for keys reused with different amounts.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c21-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C21-10 · A timed full answer</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 20-minute answer to: &quot;Explain how a ride-hailing app shows a driver's car moving on the map in real time, and what could go wrong.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. State assumptions aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Label what you assume.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 20-minute budget in section 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The technical-fluency answer structure, scored against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Path: the driver's phone reads its location every few seconds and sends it to the server (battery and data cost, privacy); the server stores the latest location in a fast store, not the main database, since only the most recent point matters; the rider's app needs updates: either it asks every few seconds (polling) or the server pushes updates over a held-open connection (push); push is smoother and cheaper at scale and needs connection management. Smoothing on the client (drawing between points) hides gaps. Constraint: freshness of a few seconds, latency under about a second, and millions of updates per second at scale (throughput). Tradeoffs: update frequency against battery and cost (every 4 seconds against every 1 second is four times the messages); accuracy against privacy (do not show exact location of a rider to the driver before pickup). What can go wrong: poor network (the car seems to jump or freeze, so show a &quot;reconnecting&quot; state and the last known time), stale data (show how old it is), GPS error in tall buildings, the queue backing up in a surge (drop old points, keep the newest), and abuse (limit who can see a driver's location and for how long). Measures: percentile of location age at display, error rate of updates, battery use per trip, support contacts about the map.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start from the user's view: what does the rider need to know (arrival time) and design the update rate for that, not for smooth animation.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Higher frequency makes the map smoother and costs battery, data and money.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: the path and plain explanations.</em> Use IQ-C21-01, IQ-C21-02, EX-C21-01, EX-C21-02 and EX-C21-03. Draw the request path from memory, and explain three terms aloud in 90 seconds each.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: data, caching and reliability.</em> Use IQ-C21-04, IQ-C21-05, EX-C21-04, EX-C21-05, EX-C21-06 and EX-C21-09. Do the percentile, cache and availability arithmetic by hand, and read a query aloud with the checklist.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: design choices and the full answer.</em> Use IQ-C21-03, IQ-C21-06, IQ-C21-07, EX-C21-07, EX-C21-08 and EX-C21-10. Practice the on-device versus cloud trade, read a design document with the ten questions, and run a full 20-minute answer with a partner who asks &quot;why is that slow?&quot; midway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to play an engineer who says &quot;it's complicated&quot; and a stakeholder who asks &quot;why can't we just cache it?&quot; Practice asking the focused question and giving the plain explanation.</p>\n<p><strong>Sit with an engineer.</strong> Ask one engineer on your team to walk you through a request in your product, hop by hop, with real timings, and note one thing that surprised you.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I describe the request path hop by hop and say where time and failure concentrate?</li>\n<li>Did I use percentiles and a stated target?</li>\n<li>Did I explain terms plainly and correctly, and say when I was unsure?</li>\n<li>Did I ask how fresh the data must be before accepting a cache?</li>\n<li>Did I check what happens if a request is sent twice?</li>\n<li>Did I check a joined total against a known number?</li>\n<li>Did I compound dependency availability and tie targets to cost?</li>\n<li>Did I compare on-device and cloud on more than one factor?</li>\n<li>Did I ask engineering focused questions, and not tell them how to build?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the hops or tradeoffs covered, the number you used, your score out of ten and the term you got wrong or avoided.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Ask an engineer to walk you through one request in your product (a page load or a core action), with timings. Draw the path (FW-C21-01) and note which hop dominates at the 95th percentile, and what the user sees when each hop fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Find a number you rely on (a dashboard metric) and trace where it comes from: the event, the pipeline and the transformation, and its freshness. Reconcile it with a second source. Write down any gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Read one query that produces a number you use, with the checklist in FW-C21-04. Ask the analyst to confirm the grain and the join behavior, and check the total against a trusted figure.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Read one design document for a project in your area with the ten questions (FW-C21-02) and pick the two most useful. Ask them in the review and note what you learned.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Look up your product's reliability targets (or ask for them): what are they, how much downtime do they allow, what is the error budget status, and what is the dependency chain?</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> For one feature that handles personal data, write a one-page comparison of where it is processed (device, server, partner), what leaves the device and why, and the retention. Review it with privacy or security.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the request-path map with the dominant hop (a time you understood a system well enough to find the problem), the reconciled number (a time you checked data before trusting it), and the design-document questions (a time you caught a risk early). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c21-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c21-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Technical fluency means knowing the shape of a system well enough to judge feasibility, cost and risk and to ask good questions; it does not mean writing code, and it includes saying what you do not know.</li>\n<li>Think in the request path: a user action crosses the device, name lookup, network, server, data store and back; time and failure concentrate in particular hops, and the experience is read at percentiles, not averages.</li>\n<li>Know the contracts and the data: an API and a schema are hard to change, retries need idempotency, joins can repeat rows, and a number's pipeline and freshness decide what it can tell you.</li>\n<li>Every architecture pattern is a tradeoff: a cache trades freshness for speed, a queue trades immediacy for resilience, services trade simplicity for independence; ask what each buys, costs and who owns each side.</li>\n<li>Reliability and privacy are product decisions with numbers: availability compounds across dependencies, an SLO with an error budget turns reliability into a plan, and on-device versus cloud processing trades latency, privacy, capability, cost and offline use, often best resolved by a hybrid with clear disclosure.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c21-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Betsy Beyer and others (eds.), <em>Site Reliability Engineering</em> (2016), on service level objectives, error budgets and incident practice <code>[V: Site Reliability Engineering, O'Reilly, 2016]</code>.</li>\n<li>Martin Kleppmann, <em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> (2017), a thorough reference for databases, caching, queues and consistency <code>[V: O'Reilly, March 2017, ISBN 9781449373320]</code>.</li>\n<li>Jakob Nielsen, &quot;Response Times: The 3 Important Limits,&quot; Nielsen Norman Group, the source of the 0.1, 1 and 10 second thresholds (from his 1993 book Usability Engineering, building on Miller, 1968) <code>[V: Nielsen, Usability Engineering (1993), chapter 5, republished by Nielsen Norman Group; thresholds trace to Miller (1968)]</code>.</li>\n<li>Joel Spolsky, &quot;The Law of Leaky Abstractions,&quot; Joel on Software, 11 November 2002 <code>[V: Joel Spolsky, Joel on Software, 11 November 2002]</code>.</li>\n<li>Peter Deutsch and others, the &quot;fallacies of distributed computing,&quot; as discussed in engineering references <code>[V: Fallacies of distributed computing: first four from Bill Joy and Tom Lyon, three more from L. Peter Deutsch (1994), the eighth from James Gosling (about 1997), all at Sun Microsystems]</code>.</li>\n<li>Gene Amdahl, &quot;Validity of the Single Processor Approach to Achieving Large Scale Computing Capabilities,&quot; <em>AFIPS Conference Proceedings</em> 30 (1967), pp. 483 to 485, and any standard summary of Amdahl's law <code>[V: AFIPS Conference Proceedings 30, Spring Joint Computer Conference, 18 to 20 April 1967, pp. 483 to 485 (IEEE reprint)]</code>.</li>\n<li>Meta, &quot;More details about the October 4 outage&quot; (5 October 2021; engineering.fb.com/2021/10/05/networking-traffic/outage-details/), and Cloudflare's analysis of the same event (blog.cloudflare.com/october-2021-facebook-outage). The sources for CS-C21-02.</li>\n<li>Your own company's published API documentation and status page, as examples of contracts and of how incidents are explained.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c22\"></span>\n<h2>C22 — PM-Level System Design</h2>\n<span id=\"c22-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Design a service that helps people find lost devices without exposing their location to others.&quot; &quot;Design the notifications system for a social app.&quot; &quot;How would you build 'where is my order'?&quot; The system design question asks you to design a product-and-technology system out loud. For a PM it is not a test of distributed-systems knowledge. It tests whether you can turn a vague product request into requirements, estimate scale well enough to choose between designs, sketch the parts and the flow of data, state the tradeoffs among speed, freshness, cost and privacy, anticipate what breaks and who might abuse it, and explain the result to engineers and to executives. Candidates fail it in two opposite ways: by diving into boxes and databases before knowing what the product must do, and by staying so abstract that nothing could be built.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: system design or technical-design questions for PM roles at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, more often for technical-leaning roles and in later rounds, usually 30 to 45 minutes <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. For associate roles the bar is a coherent, sensible design with good questions, not engineering depth. On the job it is the work of design reviews: framing the problem so engineers can design well, and checking that the design serves users.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your design is driven by the user and the constraints <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for requirements asked before components are drawn; a scale estimate that sizes the problem; a clear picture of the main data flow; named tradeoffs with a reasoned choice; failure modes with what the user sees; abuse cases where the system touches people or money; and an explanation that a non-engineer could follow.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You clarify who the users are, what the system must do, the scale and the constraints before drawing anything.</li>\n<li>You estimate load and storage with a stated method, and use the result to decide.</li>\n<li>You sketch the components and the flow of a read and a write, at a level a PM can defend.</li>\n<li>You name the tradeoffs (latency, freshness, cost, privacy) and choose with a reason.</li>\n<li>You describe failure modes, degraded behavior and abuse cases, and what the user sees.</li>\n<li>You summarize the design for engineers and for an executive, and say what you would validate first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end system design answers: starting with technology, no numbers, boxes with no data flow, &quot;it's scalable&quot; with no reasoning, no thought of failure or abuse, and an answer that engineers could not act on or executives could not follow.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-1-what-the-question-tests-and-what-it-does-not\"></span>\n<h4>C22.1 What the question tests, and what it does not</h4>\n<p>A PM-level design answer is a product decision expressed as a system. It sits between two other kinds of question. <em>Product design</em> (C07) asks what to build for whom. <em>Technical fluency</em> (C21) asks whether you understand how systems work. System design joins them: given what to build, how would the pieces fit, what does it cost, and what could go wrong? You are not expected to choose a database engine or design a sharding scheme; you are expected to know that such choices exist and to ask engineering the right questions about them.</p>\n<p><strong>What to do and what not to do.</strong> Do ask clarifying questions, state assumptions, draw a simple diagram, use numbers, name tradeoffs and failure modes, and say what you would ask an engineer. Do not recite technologies to sound expert, claim certainty about details you do not know, or build a design that ignores the user. &quot;I would ask the infrastructure team whether a relational store handles this write volume, because I estimate about 9,000 writes a second at peak&quot; is a better answer than naming a product.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use a full design.</strong> If the interviewer asks a narrow question (how would you speed up this page?), use C21's tools. Use the full structure when the prompt is &quot;design X&quot; and the system has users, data, scale and failure consequences.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked to design a notifications system, a candidate says: &quot;I'd use a message queue, a microservice for each channel, and a NoSQL database, with Kubernetes for scaling.&quot; The interviewer asks how many notifications a day and what happens at 9 a.m. when everyone's digest is due. The candidate has not asked who the users are or estimated the load, and cannot say what the technology choices are for. A stronger start: &quot;Before drawing anything: who sends and who receives, roughly how many a day, how fast must they arrive, and what must never happen, such as sending someone the same notification ten times?&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>Technology theater:</em> naming tools without reasons reads as memorization. <em>Skipping the user:</em> a design that is technically neat and ignores privacy, abuse or the user's wait fails the product test.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-2-answer-architecture-for-45-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>C22.2 Answer architecture for 45 minutes</h4>\n<p>FW-C22-01 sets out the spine. A 45-minute round divides roughly as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify the problem</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Users, the core job, what is in and out of scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Requirements</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Functional and non-functional requirements, constraints, success measures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scale estimate</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Users, requests per second at average and peak, storage, one cost figure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-level design</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Components and the data flow for the main read and write paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deep dive</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>One or two parts that matter most, with tradeoffs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure, abuse and degradation</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>What breaks, what the user sees, who might misuse it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measures, rollout and summary</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>How you would know it works, a staged launch, a closing summary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For 30 minutes shorten the clarifying and estimation steps and keep one deep dive. Say the plan aloud at the start (&quot;I'll clarify, estimate, sketch, go deep on one part, then cover failure and abuse&quot;) so the interviewer can steer.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-3-requirements-first-users-scale-constraints\"></span>\n<h4>C22.3 Requirements first: users, scale, constraints</h4>\n<p>Requirements are what the design must satisfy, and the questions you ask to find them are part of the answer (FW-C22-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Users and the core job.</strong> Who uses it (consumers, businesses, other systems)? What is the main thing they want to do, in one sentence? Who else is affected (recipients, bystanders, regulators)?</p>\n<p><strong>Functional requirements.</strong> What must the system do? Keep the list short and ordered: for a notification system, send a message to a user on one or more channels, respect the user's preferences, and avoid duplicates. Name what is out of scope for now (a non-goal, C16.2), such as an editor for message templates.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-functional requirements.</strong> How well must it do it? <em>Latency</em> (how fast must a notification arrive: seconds, minutes?), <em>freshness</em> (how current must the data be?), <em>availability</em> (what must not go down, C21.7), <em>durability</em> (what must never be lost, such as a payment record), <em>consistency</em> (must everyone see the same thing at once?), <em>cost</em> (a budget per user or per request), and <em>privacy and security</em> (what must stay secret, who must not see what).</p>\n<p><strong>Scale.</strong> How many users, how many actions per user per day, how much data, how bursty? You may be given numbers; if not, state assumptions and ask whether they are reasonable.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Regulation (data location, consent), devices and networks (C21.9), existing systems, team size and time, and what the system depends on.</p>\n<p><strong>Success measures.</strong> What would tell you it works? A primary metric tied to the user's job, with guardrails (C05).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> Requirements turn an open prompt into a bounded problem and show judgment. <strong>When not to ask everything.</strong> Limit yourself to the five or six questions whose answers change the design, and state assumptions for the rest.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-4-components-and-data-flow\"></span>\n<h4>C22.4 Components and data flow</h4>\n<p>A PM-level sketch has a handful of boxes and arrows (FW-C22-04). The standard parts, from C21, are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clients</strong> (apps and browsers), which send requests and show results.</li>\n<li><strong>An entry point</strong> (a load balancer or gateway) that receives requests, checks who the caller is, and limits abusive traffic (<strong>rate limiting</strong> is capping how many requests a caller may make in a period, to protect the system).</li>\n<li><strong>Application services</strong> that hold the logic: one or a few, named by what they do (&quot;notification service,&quot; &quot;feed service&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Data stores:</strong> a main database that is the system of record, perhaps a search index, a file or object store for photos and videos, and a <strong>cache</strong> for hot data.</li>\n<li><strong>A message queue</strong> that decouples fast request handling from slower work (sending, resizing, ranking).</li>\n<li><strong>External services</strong>: payment partners, push-notification providers, maps.</li>\n<li><strong>Background jobs and pipelines</strong> for batch work and analytics (C21.4).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Draw the flow, not just the boxes.</strong> Trace the main <em>write</em> path (what happens when a user does something) and the main <em>read</em> path (what happens when someone looks), and number the steps. For a photo upload: the app sends the photo to the entry point; the service stores the file in object storage and a record in the database; it puts a &quot;process this photo&quot; message on a queue; a worker makes thumbnails; a notification goes to followers. Say what is synchronous (the user waits) and what is asynchronous (the user does not).</p>\n<p><strong>Choose storage by the question asked of it, at PM depth.</strong> Records you look up by identifier and must not lose (users, orders): a relational database. Large files: object storage. Fast lookups of recent items: a cache. Free-text or filtered search: a search index. You can say, &quot;I would ask engineering to choose, but the access pattern is a lookup by user, with writes that must not be lost, which points to a database with strong guarantees.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-5-capacity-estimation\"></span>\n<h4>C22.5 Capacity estimation</h4>\n<p>Estimation sizes the problem and drives choices (C04 gave the method; FW-C22-03 is the sheet). Use a small set of formulas.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Requests per second (average)</strong> = daily users × actions per user per day ÷ 86,400.</li>\n<li><strong>Peak</strong> = average × a peak factor. Traffic is bursty: consumer products often peak at three to ten times the average, and event-driven ones (a sale, a launch, a morning digest) more <code>Inferred</code>. State the factor and why.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage</strong> = items per day × size per item × retention period (and any copies).</li>\n<li><strong>Bandwidth</strong> = requests per second × size of the response.</li>\n<li><strong>Cache size</strong> = the size of the hot set (the small share of items that draw most requests) times item size.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost</strong> = requests × cost per request, or storage × price per month.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example (invented).</strong> A messaging app has 10,000,000 daily users, each sending 20 messages a day: 200,000,000 messages a day, 2,315 a second on average; at a peak factor of 5, about 11,600 a second. At 1 KB a message, the daily storage is 200 GB; kept for a year (before any compression or copies), about 73 TB. Each of these numbers suggests an engineering question: can one database take 11,600 writes a second, or does it need to be split? Is 73 TB cheap enough to keep, or should old messages move to cheaper storage?</p>\n<p><strong>Use the numbers to choose.</strong> The point of the estimate is a decision: if peak writes are 100 a second, a single database is fine; if 100,000, the design must spread the load. If a response is 5 KB and there are 10,000 a second, bandwidth is 50 MB/s, trivial; at 5 MB (video) it is not. Say so aloud: &quot;At about 9,000 writes a second I would not assume one database is enough; I'd ask engineering.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Sanity-check</strong> against what you know (C04's two-route check). A figure that implies more messages than there are people-hours in the day is wrong.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-6-tradeoffs-latency-consistency-cost-privacy\"></span>\n<h4>C22.6 Tradeoffs: latency, consistency, cost, privacy</h4>\n<p>Most design choices trade four things (FW-C22-05). Name which two you are trading and choose with a reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Latency against freshness.</strong> Serving from a cache is fast and may be stale; computing on demand is fresh and slow (C21.5). Decide by the user: a like count can be seconds old; an account balance cannot.</p>\n<p><strong>Consistency against availability.</strong> When copies of data exist in several places, the system can insist that everyone sees the same value (and refuse to answer when it cannot be sure) or answer with what it has (and allow brief disagreement). <strong>Eventual consistency</strong> means copies may differ for a short time but will agree if no new changes arrive. Choose strong consistency where a mistake is costly (money, inventory counts at checkout) and eventual consistency where a brief difference is harmless (view counts, feed order).</p>\n<p><strong>Cost against everything.</strong> Faster, fresher, more redundant and more private all cost more. Express it as cost per user per month and compare with revenue per user.</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy against capability.</strong> What you collect and where you process it limit what you can build and what can leak (C21.8). Collecting less, processing on the device and encrypting so that even the company cannot read the data are strong privacy designs with costs in features and in the ability to investigate abuse. Say what you give up.</p>\n<p><strong>Push against pull.</strong> <em>Push</em> sends updates to the user as they happen (fast, costs connections and battery); <em>pull</em> has the user's app ask periodically (simple, delays updates, wastes calls when nothing changed). Worked example 2 shows the same choice in a feed.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-7-failure-modes-and-graceful-degradation\"></span>\n<h4>C22.7 Failure modes and graceful degradation</h4>\n<p><strong>Graceful degradation</strong> is a system's ability to keep providing a reduced but useful service when a part fails, instead of failing completely. A product page that loads without recommendations when the recommendation service is down is gracefully degraded; a blank page is not. FW-C22-06 is the table: for each component, how it can fail, what the user sees, how the system degrades, and the signal that tells you.</p>\n<p><strong>Common failures and defenses.</strong> Overload (shed load, queue work, <strong>rate limiting</strong>), a slow dependency (time out and use a fallback), duplicates from retries (idempotency, C21.3), lost messages (queues with acknowledgment and retry, and a way to reconcile), a bad deploy (staged rollout and rollback, C17), a data store outage (read-only mode from a cache), and a region outage (redundancy, at a cost). Say what the user sees in each: a stale value labeled with its age, a &quot;try again&quot; with the work saved, a queued action marked &quot;sending.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Abuse cases.</strong> An <strong>abuse-case</strong> is a way the system could be used to harm someone, analyzed like a requirement. Features that touch people, location, messages or money need an abuse pass (FW-C22-08): who might misuse it (a stalker, a scammer, a harasser, a bot), how, what harm would result, and what control reduces it (limits, friction, detection, user notification, easy removal). A location-finding service that works for owners works for stalkers unless the design prevents it, as Worked design in IQ-C22-06 shows.</p>\n<p><strong>Rehearse failure.</strong> Teams test failure on purpose (game days), and PMs should ask what has been tested and what the rollback is (C17.5). CS-C22-02 shows a system that was designed for a likely load and met an unlikely one.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Payment systems built for India show designs for weak connectivity and heavy load. The RBI launched UPI123Pay on 8 March 2022 so that feature-phone users, about 40 crore at the time, could pay in four ways that do not need a smartphone data connection: a voice call to a fixed number (IVR), a missed call to a merchant's number, a feature-phone app and proximity sound <code>[V: RBI press release of 8 March 2022, as reproduced by Taxmann and Lexplosion]</code>. UPI Lite takes a different approach, a small wallet kept on the phone so that small payments need no round trip to the bank each time <code>[V: NPCI announcement as reported by TechRadar; RBI limits as reported by Angel One]</code>. For a system-design answer, name the weakest connection you must serve, then choose a flow, a fallback and a limit for it <code>Inferred</code>. Limits and rules change; read the current ones yourself at the time you read this book <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-8-worked-designs-notifications-feed-search-payments-flow\"></span>\n<h4>C22.8 Worked designs: notifications, feed, search, payments flow, link service</h4>\n<p>Section 5 works five common designs at PM depth: a notifications system, a news feed (push against pull), search over listings, a payments flow with retries and webhooks (a <strong>webhook</strong> is a message one system sends to another's web address when an event happens, so the receiver need not keep asking), and a link-shortening service. In each, the steps of the spine appear: requirements, estimate, sketch, tradeoff, failure and abuse. Another pattern in several designs is <strong>fan-out</strong>: one action causing many deliveries or updates, such as one post reaching a million followers; it is the main cost driver in feeds and notifications (not to be confused with the join fan-out of C21.4, where rows repeat).</p>\n<span id=\"c22-c22-9-communicating-the-design-to-engineers-and-to-executive\"></span>\n<h4>C22.9 Communicating the design to engineers and to executives</h4>\n<p>The design is useful only if others can act on it (FW-C22-07). A one-page summary has: the problem and the user in one sentence; the requirements that drive the design; the estimate (the two or three numbers that matter); the sketch with the main flows; the key tradeoffs and the choices; the failure and abuse cases with mitigations; the open questions for engineering; what you would build first and how you would roll it out and measure it.</p>\n<p><em>For engineers:</em> lead with assumptions, numbers and open questions, in precise terms, and leave the how to them. <em>For executives:</em> lead with the user outcome, the risk (what could go wrong and how bad), the cost in money and time, and the decision you need; use plain language and one diagram. In an interview, finish with a 60-second summary in the executive form, then invite the interviewer to probe the engineering detail.</p>\n<span id=\"c22-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The request-path map (FW-C21-01), the engineer conversation questions (FW-C21-02), the latency budget (FW-C21-03), the architecture tradeoff table (FW-C21-05), the reliability targets sheet (FW-C21-06), the estimation protocol (FW-C04-01), the anchor ladder (FW-C04-03) and the rollout ladder (FW-C17-02) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c22-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C22-01 · The PM system-design spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a design prompt and 30 to 45 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with seven visible steps: clarify, requirements, estimate, high-level design, deep dive, failure and abuse, measures and summary.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will answer clarifying questions or accept stated assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure shows judgment and can crowd out depth on the one part that matters.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> starting with technology; no numbers; no failure or abuse pass.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the designs in section 5 and the model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Clarify → Requirements → Estimate → Design (read and write paths) → Deep dive → Failure and abuse → Measures, rollout, summary.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c22-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C22-02 · The requirements card · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a vague prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a card with the users and core job, functional requirements (ordered), non-functional requirements (latency, freshness, availability, durability, consistency, cost, privacy), scale, constraints, non-goals and success measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the five or six questions that change the design can be asked.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> more questions delay the design; stated assumptions save time.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a feature list with no non-functional requirements; no non-goals.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the cards in Worked examples 1 and 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Users and core job</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Must do</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Must not do</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latency and freshness</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Availability and durability</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scale (users, actions, peak)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy and constraints</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-goals</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Success measure and guardrail</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c22-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C22-03 · The capacity estimation sheet · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> daily users, actions per user, item sizes, retention and a peak factor.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> average and peak requests per second, daily and total storage, bandwidth, cache size and one cost figure, each with the engineering question it raises.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> inputs are stated and sanity-checked.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> rough numbers decide between designs; precision is not the aim.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> no peak factor; averages used for design; numbers with no decision attached.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> all five in section 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>average per second = users × actions ÷ 86,400; peak = average × factor; storage = items × size × retention; bandwidth = per second × response size.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c22-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C22-04 · The component and data-flow sketch · Original</h5>\n";
